From 5bfb8cef6de92a1526560ca02305780bc54ffbbe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Plantucha <plantucha@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 14:07:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] boards: raytac: mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle: warn against wrong
 board target

Building this board's applications for nrf52840dongle/nrf52840 produces an
image that flashes successfully and then hangs the SoC, with no diagnostic
output on any interface.

Both boards are nRF52840 dongles carrying the same Nordic Open bootloader and
using an identical nrfutil DFU procedure, so substituting one board target for
the other looks harmless. It is not: the Nordic nRF52840 Dongle (PCA10059)
populates DC/DC inductors and enables the DC/DC regulator, while this board
disables reg0 and runs reg1 in LDO mode. An image built for the Nordic board
switches the regulator to DC/DC on hardware that cannot supply it, and the SoC
dies before it can initialise any peripheral.

The failure is easy to misdiagnose because nrfutil reports "Device programmed"
and the dongle then goes completely silent on USB, including the bootloader.
That silence is itself the diagnostic: a bootloader that rejected an image
would stay in DFU mode and re-enumerate, so total silence means the
application was entered and then died.

Document the required board target, the regulator difference that causes it,
the symptom, and the fact that the board is recoverable, since DFU never
overwrites the bootloader.

Signed-off-by: Plantucha <plantucha@gmail.com>
---
 .../raytac/mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle/doc/index.rst | 35 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+)

diff --git a/boards/raytac/mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle/doc/index.rst b/boards/raytac/mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle/doc/index.rst
index b1fb298d76e..57649eea085 100644
--- a/boards/raytac/mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle/doc/index.rst
+++ b/boards/raytac/mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle/doc/index.rst
@@ -57,6 +57,41 @@ built in the usual way (see :ref:`build_an_application` for more details).
 Flashing
 ========
 
+.. warning::
+
+   Always build for ``raytac_mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle/nrf52840``. Do not substitute
+   ``nrf52840dongle/nrf52840``, even though both boards are nRF52840 dongles with
+   the same Nordic Open bootloader and the same DFU procedure.
+
+   The two boards configure the nRF52840 power regulator differently. The Nordic
+   nRF52840 Dongle (PCA10059) populates DC/DC inductors and enables the DC/DC
+   regulator; this board does not have them and runs the regulator in LDO mode:
+
+   .. code-block:: devicetree
+
+      /* nordic/nrf52840dongle */        /* raytac/mdbt50q_cx_40_dongle */
+      &reg0 { status = "okay"; };        &reg0 { status = "disabled"; };
+      &reg1 {                            &reg1 {
+        regulator-initial-mode =           regulator-initial-mode = <0>;
+          <NRF5X_REG_MODE_DCDC>;         };
+      };
+
+   An image built for the Nordic board therefore switches this board's regulator
+   to DC/DC on hardware that cannot supply it. The result is easy to misread as a
+   bricked device: ``nrfutil`` reports ``Device programmed``, and the dongle then
+   goes **completely silent on USB** -- no application, no LED, and no bootloader
+   either. No error is printed anywhere.
+
+   The distinguishing symptom is that the *bootloader* is silent too. A bootloader
+   that rejected an image stays in DFU mode and re-enumerates as ``1915:521f``;
+   total silence instead means control was handed to the application, which then
+   died before it could initialise any peripheral. If you see this, suspect the
+   board target rather than the packaging or the flash offset.
+
+   The board is not bricked, and cannot be bricked this way -- DFU does not touch
+   the bootloader. Press the button as described below to return to DFU mode and
+   reflash with the correct board target.
+
 The board supports the following programming options:
 
 1. Using the built-in bootloader only
-- 
2.53.0

